  "content": {
    "id": "p3-q8-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "8",
    "prompt": "Consider a right-angled triangle.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p3-q8a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Prove that the hypotenuse is always the longest side.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$h=\\sqrt{a^2+o^2}>a$ and $h>o$, since both legs are positive.",
          "worked": "Let the legs have lengths $a,o>0$ and the hypotenuse have length $h$. By Pythagoras,\n$$\\begin{align*}h&=\\sqrt{a^2+o^2}\\\\h&>\\sqrt{a^2}=a\\\\h&>\\sqrt{o^2}=o\\end{align*}$$\nThus the hypotenuse is longer than either leg.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 32
      },
      {
        "id": "p3-q8b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Suppose that the triangle also contains an acute angle $\\theta$. Prove that $0<\\cos\\theta<1$ and $0<\\sin\\theta<1$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$0<\\cos\\theta=\\frac ah<1$ and $0<\\sin\\theta=\\frac oh<1$, because $0<a<h$ and $0<o<h$.",
          "worked": "Relative to the acute angle $\\theta$, let $a$ and $o$ be the adjacent and opposite legs. Part (a) gives $0<a<h$ and $0<o<h$. Dividing by $h>0$ gives\n$$0<\\cos\\theta=\\frac ah<1,\\qquad 0<\\sin\\theta=\\frac oh<1.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 28
      }
    ]
  },
